 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. Kiosks have no network route during normal operation, so every lookup is answered from files on the kiosk.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface and never sees registry storage.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index, built and owned entirely inside the registry component. The snapshot file is the source of truth; the index is derived data that the registry validates and rebuilds whenever a snapshot arrives. Snapshot validation, index construction, and lookup all stay inside this component.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness on the lookup key — the same normalization that lookup applies (see Lookup Behavior) — so two records can never collide at query time.
+
+Build the replacement index completely in a temporary location; nothing about the incoming snapshot is visible while this runs. Activate the snapshot and its index together in a single step only after validation and index construction both succeed. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and index active and reports a useful error identifying what was rejected.
+
+## Index Lifecycle
+
+The index lives in kiosk-local storage at a path only the registry component reads or writes. It is derived data: if it is missing or fails to open at startup, the registry rebuilds it from the retained active snapshot before serving lookups, with no technician action. The index carries the schema version and snapshot date of the snapshot it was built from.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters on both sides of the match — when building the index's serial key at import, and on the requested serial at query time. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. A lookup that begins before an activation completes against the previous snapshot; a lookup that begins after activation uses the new one; no lookup observes a mixture. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Performance
+
+An indexed point lookup over ~90,000 rows sits far inside the 250 ms budget on a 4 GB kiosk. Index size is modest relative to the snapshot itself, so rebuild storage fits alongside the retained snapshots within kiosk disk limits.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index inside the registry component | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | The HLD requires all lookup from kiosk files with no runtime network, which eliminates both network-backed options; a local index gives predictable lookups with no service to run, and each kiosk's validate-and-rebuild cost stays inside this component. |
+| Index status | Derived data, rebuilt from the retained snapshot | Index as the primary record store | A corrupted or missing index recovers without technician action; the signed snapshot remains the only source of truth. |
+| Uniqueness key | Normalized serial (trimmed, ASCII-uppercased) | Raw serial as stored | Lookup normalizes before matching, so uniqueness on raw serials alone could let two records collide at query time. |
+| Activation | Build completely off to the side, then swap snapshot and index together | In-place update of the live index | A partially imported snapshot must never be visible; an in-place update would expose intermediate state. |
+
